A||||10||Uaana||Grand Am Brake warning.||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||07-10-2001||12:24 AM||||I know alot of people that just love this upgrade. But I thought I'd just pass on a little info.<BR>I've been hearing a "clunking" noise out of the front end for the last couple of weeks. Inspected everything, tie rods, ball joints ect. the only thing off was the wheel bearings. Looks like the hubs were not machined properly and were tearing apart the bearings and the hub assembly. I have now gone back to stock and everything is roses.<P>Keep in mind if you have this done that your machining for the inner hubs is dead on and balanced. See if you can find a shop that will mate the new rotors to the hubs.. might save you some hassle in the future.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric_PA||07-10-2001||06:34 AM||||I have the same noise up front anytime I come to a stop. This has started a few months after I completed my Grand am brake upgrade. I checked and replaced everything in the front end, including new poly. I am going to check the hubs on mine, if that's the case, I'm switching back. I didn't fabricate anything to do this swap since I purchased it from the Fiero store. I must say, I'm not that happy with this particular brake upgrade.... The pedal is way too mushy.<BR>||||reg||11
Z||000002||FieroBUZZ||07-10-2001||07:45 AM||||Just a thought, but there seem to be enough of us who want to do an upgrade to GA specs. Could we not do a group manufacture/purchase through one of the aftermarket hot rod brake companies for a rotor that is thicker(vented) and still accepts our bearings, etc?<BR>The rest of the upgrade seems pretty DIY.<BR>Gary||||reg||8
Z||000003||terryk||07-10-2001||02:22 PM||||I switched back and I'm glad I did.||||reg||1
Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||07-10-2001||04:22 PM||||mushy?? how could anything be mushier than the stock brakes??<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||11
Z||000005||theogre||07-10-2001||08:23 PM||||In the brake upgrade math thread, at least I think it's that one, someone with machinist skills explained the issues with the fit of the Grand Am hub and balance and all that.<P>A big issue is that you can not reliably use any point on the rotor for centering except it center hole. (If you machine the rotor so the hat can be used you risk starting cracks in the hat...)<P>These problems can't be fully fixed with just a diferant rotor. It needs additional changes to the OE hub or a new hub.<P>The flange face of the OE hub needs to be cut back some to reduce, eliminate if posible, the amount the wheel is pushed outboard. Durring this machine work a proper centering collar can be cut to keep the rotor in place.<P>If the OE hub can tolerate the needed cutting then you need a rotor that matches the Grand Am rotor in all dimensions except one. You'll need a shorter "hat."<P>Wilwood, Baer, and probably a few other places could make such a rotor. It will not be cheap. You probably wouldn't it if it was. You definatly pay for quality work...<P>Another posibility is to dig thru the brake catalogs and see if there is an alternate one available off the shelf. I've saw in one of the brake upgrade articles (Not a grand am one.) that Car Quest has a catalog with lots of pictures and specs. Other parts shops may have a similar book. <P>You'd need to look up all the specs for GA rotor and then dig untill you maybe find another one or two that can replace it. The exact one will depend on what amount of cutting the hub face will tolerate. Very Time consuming but a hell of allot cheaper.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric_PA||07-10-2001||08:50 PM||||<BR>"mushy?? how could anything be mushier than the stock brakes?" <BR>I was at work when I replied.. the boss was getting to me..<P>I should take that back.. What I meant to say was that there is a lot of break pedal travel, but there is indeed less brake fade with this setup compared to stock. That is of course my own observation, others may have other opinions on this. I am going to switch back, just because I don't feel safe with the setup on my car. I may have it setup wrong, but I am no longer going to mess with the brake system. Maybe after everything else is done with the car I will look for something like a Wild Wood setup.||||reg||1
Z||000007||sparks||07-11-2001||02:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Eric_PA:<BR><B>I have the same noise up front anytime I come to a stop. This has started a few months after I completed my Grand am brake upgrade. I checked and replaced everything in the front end, including new poly. I am going to check the hubs on mine, if that's the case, I'm switching back. I didn't fabricate anything to do this swap since I purchased it from the Fiero Store. I must say, I'm not that happy with this particular brake upgrade.... The pedal is way too mushy.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is this the "vented brake" upgrade that they sell??<P><P>------------------<BR><BR>Adam Sparks||||reg||1
Z||000008||jelly2m8||07-11-2001||08:07 PM|||| Sounds like those of you having problems with the GA brake conversion, are either having improperly machined hubs, or have installation issues. <P> I have the GA brakes on the front of my fastback for 2 years now, and haven't had any problem yet. I have takin the car on a 2000 mile road trip with no problems, I run it up to 130mph-ish fairly often, and brake hard enough that I can smell the brakes on all 4 corners for 5 minutes after...<P> I wouldnt knock the GA brakes, untill you verify what each of your particular problem is.<P> The V8 is soon ready for brakes, and I have no hesitation about putting the GA brakes on it.<P> There are several of use on the Fiero Owners Online chatroom that use the GA barkes, and no one has made any complant's.||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||07-11-2001||11:52 PM||||Compare for a second the cost of a "GA" swap on all 4 corners...<P>Add the loss of parking brake.<P>Add the fact that your life and maybe mine depend on your brakes.<P>Now look here: <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>Way cool cheap life insurance!!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||lowCG||07-12-2001||10:49 PM||||My future brake setup is finally under way,that is my original hub/rotors are at the machine shop being reduced to just hubs with a 5" OD.I'm not,and had never planned to cut down the face of the hubs because the rotors I'm using register snug to the center of the stock hub,can't imagine why one would do anything to potentially weaken a hub on a mid-engined car(?)<BR>The car will be fine with an extra 1/2" added to the front track,and 1/4" longer wheel studs.<BR>The Subaru Legacy rotors have a large enough hat,or setback to them to allow me to use these wide,cast iron BMW/Volvo calipers and not contact the wheel.The reason I'm not going to use Wilwoods is that they have short pistons,and I've heard that they don't have dust seals.Basically I want bullet-proof,low maintenance bigger brakes,and 4 piston calipers.<BR>I saw a set of Brembo Porsche 944 Turbo front calipers on Ebay yesterday($300),and would try to buy them,but I already rebuilt mine.These calipers aren't huge like on 930 Turbos,but are very good.<p>[This message has been edited by lowCG (edited 07-12-2001).]||||reg||1
